# Scope for `semgrep ci` / `semgrep scan`. Anchored paths are repo-root
# relative (gitignore syntax) — keep the leading `/` so they cannot match
# a same-named directory deeper in the tree.
#
# NOTE: a project .semgrepignore REPLACES Semgrep's built-in default
# ignore list — the first block below re-declares those defaults
# verbatim so nothing loses coverage. Repo-specific entries follow.

# --- Semgrep default template --------------------------------------
# Administrative folder or file used by popular version control systems
.git
.svn
.hg
_darcs
CVS

# Paths to files and folders that are typically large and ignorable
build/
vendor/
dist/
*.min.js
.env/
.tox/

# Package managers
node_modules/
.npm/
.yarn/
.venv/
_opam/
_build/
_cargo/

# Common test paths — test scaffolding holds fixtures/intentional
# bad patterns by design; findings there are noise.
test/
tests/
testsuite/
*_test.go

# --- Repo-specific --------------------------------------------------
# Rationale: findings outside cmd/+internal/ are operator tooling and
# generated artifacts that never see untrusted input. The real attack
# surface stays scanned: cmd/, internal/, .github/, package-lock.json.

# Vendored third-party UI assets (bulma/chart.js/alpine-csp) — pinned
# upstream code, same class as node_modules; scanning it is pure noise.
/internal/server/static/vendor/

# Committed release binaries + checksums — build output, not source.
/dist/

# Generated scan reports (--html output) and scratch assets.
/report/
/static-tmp/

# Coverage/test artifacts.
/coverage.out
/test-report.json
